{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":2,"output":5,"cacheRead":10568,"cacheWrite":2784,"totalTokens":13359,"cost":{"input":2.0000000000000002e-7,"output":0.0000024999999999999998,"cacheRead":0.00010568,"cacheWrite":0.000348,"total":0.00045638,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"I'm checking for the ADRs, which turn out to be absent, so I'll follow the repo's usual conventions: a `__rich_console__` generator that yields Segments, a `__rich_measure__` that returns a Measurement, `cell_len` for width, and no touching `console._buffer`. I'm weighing whether to build a Text object and render it, but I'd rather yield Segments directly and truncate them to `options.max_width`.\n\nI'm settling on the badge layout now: the content gets wrapped in \"( \" and \" )\" for pill borders and \"[ \" and \" ]\" for square ones. I'm leaning toward building a Text object with styled spans and rendering it through the console, so wrapping and cell width are handled for me, rather than hand-cropping raw Segments.\n\nI'm sizing the badge by adding up the cell widths of the left border, content, and right border, then returning a fixed Measurement and letting the caller clamp it to the available width. I'm also planning to make the Text non-wrapping with ellipsis overflow so it stays on one line and crops cleanly when space is tight.\n\nI'm checking the ordering of Text.assemble parts, which takes (str, style) pairs, and that style strings get resolved at render time through the console, so they can stay as plain strings. I'm also deciding whether Badge should inherit JupyterMixin like Constrain does, and I'm leaning toward including it for consistency.\n\nI'm working out how to export Badge from the top-level rich package without causing import trouble, since the new module pulls in text handling. I'm checking whether rich.text leads back into rich.console, which would decide whether a top-level import is safe or needs to be deferred.\n\nI'm checking how `rich/__init__.py` pulls in its other modules so I can add `Badge` to the top-level imports, and I'll run an import test afterward to make sure there's no circularity. I'm also planning to extend `__all__` with \"Badge\" and to add a small test module alongside the component.\n\n"}}
